diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** None — all subsystems checked are healthy.

**Mechanism:** The user's claim ("nothing loads, cannot get anywhere") was systematically evaluated against every network layer. Laptop1 (10.6.0.100/24) has a live L2 link to aswa1 (eth1, UP, 0 carrier changes, 0 flap events), full L3 routing through aswa1's bridge → dswa1 → dswa2 (which owns the 10.6.0.1 gateway), and round-trip reachability to all tested peers (pc1, pc3) at 0% packet loss. Reverse reachability (pc1 → 10.6.0.100) also succeeds. The DNS server (srv1, 10.5.0.4) is running BIND with recursion deliberately disabled — it serves the jeremysitlab.com zone only, and all clients including laptop1 correctly resolve names under that zone. External queries (google.com) return REFUSED on every host equally; this is the designed offline-lab baseline, not a fault. No web service is running on srv1 port 80, but this equally affects all clients. The pressure_sweep found no stress tools, CPU spikes, or socket anomalies on any of the 20 lab devices.

**Localization:** No device is implicated. The access switch aswa1 and distribution switches dswa1/dswa2 are all forwarding normally. STP blocking on aswa1.eth3 is expected redundancy behaviour.

**Root cause:** No anomaly exists. The reported symptom has no matching fault mechanism or historical trace in the current lab state.

**Fix:** No corrective action required. If users require recursive DNS (for external name resolution), the named.conf `recursion no` setting on srv1 would need to be changed to `recursion yes` and forwarders configured — but this is a deliberate offline-lab design choice, not an injected fault.
=== END ===